<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">23 December in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">4 events that happened on 23 December, from 1783 to 1947.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-washington.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1783 CE <span class="tih-ago">243 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">George Washington resigns his military command</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> State House at Annapolis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> returned his commission to Congress. A victorious general giving up power voluntarily set a lasting precedent for civilian control of the military.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1815 CE <span class="tih-ago">211 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Jane Austen's Emma is published</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">John Murray published Emma, though its title page bore the date 1816. Austen's comedy of a confident young matchmaker is often judged her most accomplished novel.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1888 CE <span class="tih-ago">138 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Vincent van Gogh cuts off part of his ear</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After a quarrel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-gauguin">Paul Gauguin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-van-gogh">Vincent van Gogh</a> cut off part of his left ear. Months later he entered the asylum at Saint-Rémy, where he painted The Starry Night.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1947 CE <span class="tih-ago">79 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Bell Labs demonstrates the transistor</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">John Bardeen and Walter Brattain showed colleagues at Bell Laboratories their point-contact transistor amplifying a signal. Transistors replaced vacuum tubes and made modern electronics possible.</p></div></li></ol>
